# Line endings.
#
# The rule that matters is the second one. A shell script checked out with CRLF is broken on
# Unix — the shebang becomes `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, and bash reports
# `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, which reads as "bash is missing" rather than "this
# file has the wrong line endings". A Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true` would introduce
# exactly that, so `.sh` files are pinned to LF regardless of the cloning platform's settings.
#
# Everything else is marked `-text`, meaning "never convert": the repository has a mix of LF and
# CRLF files, and normalising them now would rewrite most of it for no functional gain. `-text`
# keeps every checkout byte-for-byte identical to what was committed, which is what makes the
# mix harmless rather than platform-dependent.

* -text
*.sh text eol=lf
